    <statement><p>Consider functions <m>f: {\left\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\right\}} \to \{a,b,c,d,e,f\}\text{.}</m> How many functions have the property that <m>f(1) \ne c</m> or <m>f(2) \ne f\text{,}</m> or both?</p>
<p><fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5\cdot 6^{8}+5\cdot 6^{8}-5^{2}\cdot 6^{7}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>There are <m>5 \cdot 6^{8}</m> functions for which <m>f(1) \ne c</m> and another <m>5 \cdot 6^{8}</m> functions for which <m>f(2) \ne f\text{.}</m>  There are <m>5^2 \cdot 6^{7}</m> functions for which both <m>f(1) \ne c</m> and <m>f(2) \ne f\text{.}</m>  So the total number of functions for which <m>f(1) \ne c</m> or <m>f(2) \ne f</m> or both is</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to center align here. -->
<p>
<m>\displaystyle{                5 \cdot 6^{8}+ 5 \cdot 6^{8} - 5^2 \cdot 6^{7} = {9.79776\times 10^{6}}.
              }</m></p>
</solution>
